<Project>

  <!--
    AppxMSBuildToolsPath
    =====================
    Why this exists: WindowsAppSDK's PRI/Appx build targets (MrtCore.PriGen.targets,
    Microsoft.Build.Msix.Packaging.targets) need Microsoft.Build.Packaging.Pri.Tasks.dll and
    Microsoft.Build.AppxPackage.dll even for a WindowsPackageType=None class library like
    WinUIDesigner.XamlDom - by design, WindowsAppSDK always wires GetPriOutputs into the
    normal output-group chain whenever UseWinUI=true. Those DLLs only ship inside a Visual
    Studio install, under
      <VS install>\MSBuild\Microsoft\VisualStudio\v<version>\AppxPackage\
    WindowsAppSDK defaults AppxMSBuildToolsPath to
      $(MSBuildExtensionsPath)\Microsoft\VisualStudio\v$(VisualStudioVersion)\AppxPackage\
    but under `dotnet build`/`dotnet test`, $(MSBuildExtensionsPath) resolves to the
    standalone .NET SDK's own tree, which never has an AppxPackage folder at all - hence
    MSB4062 ("task could not be loaded from the assembly ...\AppxPackage\...") unless
    AppxMSBuildToolsPath is pointed at an actual VS install explicitly.

    This used to be a literal, machine-specific path
    (`...\Microsoft Visual Studio\18\Professional\...`) duplicated in both csproj files -
    portable to exactly one machine/edition and out of sync risk between the two files.
    Centralized here instead, and resolved dynamically via vswhere.exe (ships with every
    VS 2017+ install at a stable path) so it adapts to whatever VS edition/version is
    actually installed.

    Resolution order (first that applies wins):
      1. AppxMSBuildToolsPathOverride - manual escape hatch. Set as an environment variable
         or an MSBuild global property (`-p:AppxMSBuildToolsPathOverride=...`) to bypass
         vswhere entirely, e.g. on a machine/CI agent with a non-standard layout.
      2. vswhere.exe's answer, cached to a small generated file (see the
         _ResolveAppxMSBuildToolsPath target below) and appended with the fixed
         `MSBuild\Microsoft\VisualStudio\v18.0\AppxPackage\` suffix.
      3. Left unset. If vswhere can't find a suitable install, AppxMSBuildToolsPath stays
         empty and WindowsAppSDK's own default kicks in, reproducing the original, clear
         MSB4062 error - fail loud, not silently wrong.

    Why a generated-file cache instead of just running vswhere from an MSBuild target:
    WindowsAppSDK's own AppxMSBuildToolsPath default is consumed by
    <UsingTask AssemblyFile="..."> elements, which MSBuild resolves during *evaluation*,
    before any target (including one in this file) has run. An <Exec> called from an
    ordinary build-time target would set the property far too late to affect that
    resolution. Restore runs as its own earlier, separate MSBuild evaluation+execution pass
    before the main Build/Test evaluation begins, so a target hooked into Restore can run
    vswhere and write its result to disk in time for the next evaluation (this file, via the
    <Import> below) to read it back in as a plain, already-known property value.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <_AppxToolsPathCacheFile>$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\obj\_AppxMSBuildToolsPath.g.props</_AppxToolsPathCacheFile>
    <_VsWhereExe>$(MSBuildProgramFiles32)\Microsoft Visual Studio\Installer\vswhere.exe</_VsWhereExe>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="$(_AppxToolsPathCacheFile)" Condition="Exists('$(_AppxToolsPathCacheFile)')" />

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(AppxMSBuildToolsPathOverride)' != ''">
    <AppxMSBuildToolsPath>$(AppxMSBuildToolsPathOverride)</AppxMSBuildToolsPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="_ResolveAppxMSBuildToolsPath"
          BeforeTargets="Restore;CollectPackageReferences"
          Condition="'$(AppxMSBuildToolsPathOverride)' == '' and Exists('$(_VsWhereExe)')">
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(_VsWhereExe)&quot; -latest -property installationPath"
          ConsoleToMSBuild="true"
          StandardOutputImportance="low"
          ContinueOnError="WarnAndContinue">
      <Output TaskParameter="ConsoleOutput" ItemName="_VsWhereOutputLines" />
    </Exec>

    <PropertyGroup>
      <_VsInstallPath>@(_VsWhereOutputLines)</_VsInstallPath>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_VsInstallPath)' != ''">
      <_AppxToolsPathCacheLines Include="&lt;Project&gt;" />
      <_AppxToolsPathCacheLines Include="  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;" />
      <_AppxToolsPathCacheLines Include="    &lt;AppxMSBuildToolsPath&gt;$(_VsInstallPath)\MSBuild\Microsoft\VisualStudio\v18.0\AppxPackage\&lt;/AppxMSBuildToolsPath&gt;" />
      <_AppxToolsPathCacheLines Include="  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;" />
      <_AppxToolsPathCacheLines Include="&lt;/Project&gt;" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(_AppxToolsPathCacheFile)"
                       Lines="@(_AppxToolsPathCacheLines)"
                       Overwrite="true"
                       Condition="'$(_VsInstallPath)' != ''" />
  </Target>

</Project>
